WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.810
Pixar's movies are a veritable basket of Easter
eggs, filled with running gags and references

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:10.290
to other Pixar properties that only eagle-eyed
fans might notice.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:14.590
Cars 3 is no exception, and contains a truckload
of new hidden gems.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:16.439
"But I like a challenge."

00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:20.460
Here's a look at some of the Easter eggs you
might've missed in the Cars franchise's third

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.710
lap around the track.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:23.949
A113

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:29.050
It's a tradition for Pixar films to contain
at least one reference to A113, the number

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.550
of the classroom in which many of its veterans—including
John Lasseter and Brad Bird—studied in at

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.920
the California Institute for the Arts.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.500
Luckily, the symbol wasn't too hard to spot
in Cars 3.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.350
A113 shows up most noticeably on the door
of the office of Rust-eze owner and company

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:52.130
CEO Sterling as he takes a meeting with his
new racer—er, product—Lightning McQueen.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.829
The Pizza Planet truck

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:59.090
Ever since it debuted in Pixar's first feature
film, Toy Story, the Pizza Planet truck has

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.260
popped up in most of its films.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.070
Considering Lightning McQueen's car number
95 is a direct nod to the year Toy Story was

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:12.090
released, it's not too surprising that in
Cars 3, the old pickup makes yet another appearance.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:16.740
The truck pops up this time during the demolition
derby race McQueen and his trainer-turned-protégé

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.460
Cruz Ramirez participate in while undercover.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.170
In the mud track scene, it's open season in
terms of which types of vehicles can participate

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:29.830
in the Crazy Eight run, with the last vehicle
standing emerging as the night's winner.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:34.450
And for a brief moment, the Pizza Planet truck
can be seen giving it a go against the likes

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:40.250
of the incognito McQueen, Ramirez, and school
bus Miss Fritter (whose name may be a wink

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.579
to Magic School Bus teacher Miss Frizzle).

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:44.090
"I don't know.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.619
I got nothing"

00:01:45.619 --> 00:01:47.270
The Pixar Ball

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:53.110
Much like A113, the Pixar Luxo ball—a yellow
bouncy ball with a blue stripe and a red star—has

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.360
become a must for each of the studio's films,
including Cars 3.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.869
Along with the Pizza Planet truck, the Pixar
ball can be seen in the demolition derby sequence,

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:05.760
drawn on the hood of one car that crashes
into another.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.789
Whereas the Pizza Planet truck was a dead
giveaway thanks to its rocket topper, the

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:14.489
Pixar Ball is a little trickier to spot in
the mud-slinging crowd of cars.

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:15.890
Some familiar branding

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:21.031
The Cars series is filled with automobiles
that are sponsored by faux corporations, and

00:02:21.031 --> 00:02:24.110
there are a ton of Easter eggs planted in
those brand names.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:29.040
Buy n Large, for example, makes an appearance
as the sponsor of one of the early race cars

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.950
in the film and is seen on the sidelines of
one of McQueen's major races against rookie

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.060
sensation Jackson Storm.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.530
BnL, Pixar fans might recall, is the corporation
that made an appearance in Wall-E and played

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.830
a big role in leaving the world in ruins.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:48.480
It's since appeared in subsequent Pixar pics,
including Toy Story 3 and Up.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.239
Dinoco is another old brand that's continued
to maintain its relevance in the Cars world,

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:58.349
as the sponsor of Strip Weathers, his nephew
Cal Weathers, and, eventually, newcomer speedster

00:02:58.349 --> 00:02:59.629
Ramirez.

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:04.080
Another brand that gets a nod is Triple Dent
Gum, which was first introduced in Inside

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.600
Out.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.360
Cinderella's pumpkin

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.250
Sterling's office isn't just the place where
A113 can be found—the iconic image of Cinderella's

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.890
pumpkin carriage can also be seen in the background
of his office.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:20.640
Although Cinderella is an ancient property
of pre-Pixar Disney, the film has been referenced

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.420
in subsequent House of Mouse properties before,
including The Little Mermaid.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.430
Coco

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.849
Pixar's next cinematic release, Coco, also
gets a couple of nods in Cars 3.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:35.540
Not only does the name of the film appear
to pop up in in the racetrack sidelines during

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:40.409
a few shots in the film, but the town featured
in the movie is also showcased on a training

00:03:40.409 --> 00:03:45.430
screen at the fancy new Sterling Center, when
a racer is asked by Ramirez to think of his

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:46.480
hometown.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.319
NASCAR racers take a new name

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:53.040
NASCAR fans should have a field day figuring
out which of the tertiary characters in the

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.110
film represent the real-life racers who voice
them.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.879
Among the industry superstars who make brief
audio cameos as car characters in the film:

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:06.560
Jeff Gordon reprising his Cars 2 role as Jeff
Gorvette, Darrell Waltrip coming back as Darrell

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.620
Cartrip, Daniel Suarez making his debut as
Daniel Swervez, Ryan Blaney as Ryan "Inside"

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:18.900
Laney, Chase Elliott as Chase Racelott, and
Bubba Wallace, driver of the #6 Disney Pixar

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.530
Cars 3 Ford, as Bubba Wheelhouse.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:25.180
Gentlemen, start your microphones.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:29.250
Perhaps the most subtle NASCAR reference,
though, had more to do with a monkey than

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.560
a human driver.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.770
Jocko Flocko is the storied simian co-driver
that helped Tim Flock win a race in the early

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:36.770
'50s.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.800
His name was referenced by the disguise Mack
put on his trailer to trick racers at the

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.930
dirt track where McQueen and Ramirez went
to practice.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.310
Paul Newman resurrected

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:52.080
Perhaps one of the most bittersweet surprises
in Cars 3 is the return of Doc Hudson, a character

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.780
originally voiced by the late Paul Newman.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:05:00.270
His status as Lightning's mentor made a monumental
impact on the racer, especially as Lightning

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:06.180
added more miles to his odometer and started
being edged out by newer, faster vehicles.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.150
Not only was he brought back in spirit in
flashbacks to his scenes from Cars, but he

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.380
was also given new dialogue during those nostalgic
moments.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.120
"More speed.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.900
More of everything."

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:22.060
According to Cars 3 producer Kevin Reher,
those soundbites came courtesy of between-takes

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:27.560
Cars audio that captured Newman—a real-life
driving enthusiast—talking about racing

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.750
just for fun.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:33.560
He told SlashFilm, "John [Lasseter] had left
all the tapes running on Cars 1, so we had

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:38.389
all these hours of Paul talking about everything
and a lot of it was about racing."

00:05:38.389 --> 00:05:39.710
Car Talk

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:45.580
For fans of the NPR talk show Car Talk, the
voices of Tom Magliozzi (who passed away in

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:51.370
2014) and his brother Ray have been fun additions
as Rusty and Dusty throughout the Cars films.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:56.009
They pay poignant tribute to their old show
while saying goodbye to McQueen—after selling

00:05:56.009 --> 00:06:00.590
Rust-eze to Sterling with the hopes of giving
their prized racer a better life—by uttering

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:01.960
the Car Talk catchphrase.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.710
"Don't drive like my brother.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:05.050
And don't drive like my brother!"

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.300
Thanks for watching!

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.150
